          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does COPD guidance describe about when to add an inhaled corticosteroid?",
          "answer": "The main COPD guidance I could retrieve describes adding an inhaled corticosteroid (a steroid inhaler that reduces airway inflammation) in a specific situation, but it does not give a numerical cut-off. The source is the \"COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy\" document (Global respiratory strategy report, educational summary).\n\n**What the guidance says:**\n- For people with a significant history of flare-ups (exacerbations), the starting treatment is two long-acting bronchodilators (airway-opening medicines), not a steroid.\n- An inhaled corticosteroid is added in that group when the **blood eosinophil count** is high, or when there are features of asthma alongside COPD.\n- Eosinophils are a type of white blood cell. The guidance uses the blood count as the marker for who is likely to benefit.\n- The reason for using a marker is that inhaled corticosteroids raise the risk of pneumonia in this group. The guidance says the benefit should be identifiable before the risk is accepted.\n\n**What I could not find:**\n- The retrieved text does not say what counts as a \"high\" eosinophil count. I have no number to give you, and you should not rely on one from memory.\n- The retrieved passage is cut off, so I cannot fully confirm what it says for people with high symptom burden and no significant exacerbation history.\n- The passage does not grade how strong the evidence is behind this approach.\n- The COPD document has no disagreement section. The disagreements my search returned were about asthma, osteoarthritis, diabetes screening and infections, not COPD. So I cannot report a documented COPD disagreement, though that does not mean none exists.\n\nThe asthma guidance in the same search says all adults with asthma should receive an inhaled corticosteroid. That is a different condition and does not apply to COPD.\n\nWhether an inhaled corticosteroid is right depends on your own eosinophil count, exacerbation history and other features, so please raise it with the clinician who manages your breathing. If your breathing suddenly becomes much worse, you have trouble speaking in full sentences, or your lips turn blue, seek emergency care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
